# Deliberate exceptions to bin/check-public-safe. One regexp per line, each with
# a reason. Adding a line here is a decision someone can review; bypassing the
# check with --no-verify is not.

# Rails' own generated comments and config reference 127.0.0.1 / 0.0.0.0, which
# are not host addresses of ours.
^.*(?:127\.0\.0\.1|0\.0\.0\.0).*$

# Documentation examples use example.com deliberately.
example\.com

# Rails' generated database.yml comments carry placeholder connection URLs
# (myuser:mypass, cacheuser:cachepass). Matched narrowly rather than by
# excluding comments, because a real secret in a comment is still a leak.
(?:myuser:mypass|cacheuser:cachepass)@localhost

# The CI postgres service, whose credentials are postgres/postgres on localhost
# inside a throwaway container. Matched narrowly rather than excluding the
# workflow, which is exactly where a real secret would be most dangerous.
postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost

# A commit message explaining the four-part-gem-version false positive quotes
# the versions that caused it. Allowlisted as literals rather than by relaxing
# the IP pattern, so an actual address in a message is still refused.
\b(?:3\.17\.0\.6|3\.3\.12\.0)\b

# RFC 5737 documentation address ranges (192.0.2.0/24, 198.51.100.0/24,
# 203.0.113.0/24), used in specs that record a browser's IP. These exist
# precisely so examples can carry an address that can never route, so they
# cannot describe infrastructure of ours. Matched as the three reserved /24s
# rather than by relaxing the IP pattern or excluding spec/, so a real address
# anywhere -- including in a spec -- is still refused.
\b(?:192\.0\.2|198\.51\.100|203\.0\.113)\.\d{1,3}\b
